# Strike a corporeal undead with a flame tongue sword and watch what the
# creature's hit points do over the next four game turns.
#
# Read by tools/check_flame_tongue_undead.sh, for PA-08-F11 of bd inc-tek.8.8.
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. The mummy's hit points, five times: once on the turn
# of the hit and once after each of the next four EV_TURN boundaries. The
# sword's page (lib/m_items.irh, the "flame tongue" entity) promises that a
# struck corporeal undead "catches flame and continues to burn, causing it to
# suffer 3d6 fire damage the first turn after the hit, 2d6 the second turn and
# 1d6 on the third". So the reading must fall three times and then stop.
#
# ON A BUILD WITHOUT THE FIX all five readings are the SAME number. The sword's
# EITEM(EV_HIT) handler was an empty stub -- a comment and `return NOTHING;` --
# so nothing at all followed the blow.
#
# WHY A WIZARD-MODE DUMP IS THE ORACLE. Creature::Dump prints "HP:cur/max"
# (src/Debug.cpp, Creature::Dump). Nothing else in the game shows a monster's
# hit points as a number, and the damage messages carry no figures. The
# Examine Nearby Things browser is reached from the wizard menu, walks the
# map's thing list with RIGHT (src/Debug.cpp, Player::BrowseThings), and
# spends no game time, so a reading never disturbs what it reads.
#
# WHY A MUMMY. It must be undead, corporeal, and it must survive all three
# burns or the last readings measure a corpse. The mummy is the cheapest such
# subject: the game files it under "Corporeal Undead" in the summoning menu,
# it carries M_UNDEAD and not M_INCOR, and 8d12 hit dice leave it standing.
# It is also vulnerable to fire (lib/mon3.irh, "mummy": AD_FIRE damage is
# multiplied by 1.5), which makes the three drops larger and easier to read;
# the check allows for that and never asserts an exact die total.
#
# WHY THE MONSTERS ARE FROZEN. The subject stands next to the player for about
# eighty game turns while the burn runs. A mummy that acts would kill a first
# level barbarian long before then, and a dead player measures nothing. The
# Freeze Monsters switch denies monsters their activity cycles but leaves them
# in the world (src/Main.cpp, the OPT_FREEZE_MON arm), and EV_TURN is still
# thrown at every thing on the map, so the burn still ticks.
#
# THE ARITHMETIC OF THE WAITING. Game::Turn counts ticks; EV_TURN is thrown to
# everything on the map once every 60 of them (src/Main.cpp, `if (!(Turn%60))`).
# One Search costs exactly 3 ticks here -- measured, not assumed -- so `s*21`
# is 63 ticks and crosses exactly one multiple of 60 whatever the phase. Four
# such blocks therefore step the burn exactly one turn at a time. Infinite
# Fatigue is switched on so that eighty-four Searches cannot exhaust the
# character instead.
#
# Menu letters move when a list changes. Run with the seed the check supplies.

@include chargen.keys

# --- WIZARD SWITCHES --------------------------------------------------------
# The option list is a fixed table (src/Tables.cpp, the OPC_WIZARD rows), so
# these are counted rows and not searched text: Infinite Fatigue is the fourth
# and Freeze Monsters the eleventh.
w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
DOWN*3
SPACE           # Infinite Fatigue: NO -> YES
DOWN*7
SPACE           # Freeze Monsters:  NO -> YES
@dump:switches
ESC

# --- PUT A FLAME TONGUE IN THE CHARACTER'S HAND -----------------------------
# RIGHT*10 walks the glyph row to Specific Weapons (src/Tables.cpp, ItemGlyphs).
# The candidate list has no menu letters and shows three rows with the MIDDLE
# one selected, so the walk stops on the pass where the row BELOW the wanted
# one appears -- "Javelin of Lightning" is the AI_WEAPON entity that follows
# "flame tongue" in lib/m_items.irh. The check does not trust that ordering: it
# reads the sword's name back off the inventory screen before believing any
# number.
w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*10
@until "Javelin of Lightning" DOWN
ENTER
2               # "Enter Item Level:" -- the sword's own INITIAL_PLUS
ENTER
b               # Weapon Hand; the slot lettering skips 'd', the Drop key
SPACE           # swap the sword into it
n               # decline wielding a long sword two-handed
s               # stow the orcish knife the swap pushed into the air
@dump:wielded
ESC

# --- SUMMON THE SUBJECT ONE SQUARE EAST -------------------------------------
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Corporeal Undead"
@choose "mummy"
RIGHT           # the location cursor starts on the player; one step east
ENTER
@dump:summoned

# --- HIT IT -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Walking into it is attacking it. A first level barbarian misses a defense
# class of 16 about half the time, so keep swinging until one lands. @until
# stops on the first pass that shows the phrase, so exactly one hit has landed
# when this line finishes.
@until "hitting the mummy" RIGHT
@dump:struck

# --- FIVE READINGS ----------------------------------------------------------
w
@choose "Examine Nearby Things"
@until "mummy (class" RIGHT
@dump:hp-0
ESC

s*21
w
@choose "Examine Nearby Things"
@until "mummy (class" RIGHT
@dump:hp-1
ESC

s*21
w
@choose "Examine Nearby Things"
@until "mummy (class" RIGHT
@dump:hp-2
ESC

s*21
w
@choose "Examine Nearby Things"
@until "mummy (class" RIGHT
@dump:hp-3
ESC

s*21
w
@choose "Examine Nearby Things"
@until "mummy (class" RIGHT
@dump:hp-4
ESC

@quit
